I am back. I think the next step for me is to add a duration overlay, as knowing the duration of a video matters a lot for me.
I am back again and I have implemented the duration overlay. Also, I just learned what a z-index is! Basically what was happening to me was this- I had two relative-absolute pairs of elements in Genre.svelte and Video.svelte. It seems that the browser paints the "screen" based on the order of declaration, so the scroll button on the right was rendering correctly (as it was the last declaration), but because my left button was declared before the Video components, it was getting overshadowed by a video, literally, as the video's own relative-absolute pair was covering it. As it turns out, there are 3 indexes in CSS. The x-y index is what we work with when we position an element. In this case, my left arrow button and the video component both have the same relative-absolute logic, so that means that their x-y (moving left-right and up-down) index was the same. Where they could be truly differrent was the z-index. This means how "close" an element is to the user - and yes, this is a 3D visualisation. It was how well you could "stack up" these elements. See stacking context at MDN: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Positioned_layout/Stacking_context.
So yeah, in my case I wanted the button to "win" or be "closer" to the user, so I just put a z-10 on it which made its z-index higher than the other relative-absolute pair and hence it was shown to the user first. Note that both were primed to have a z-index as both had relative-absolute, the z-index only works if you have some position on the target, I think.
But yeah, anyways, that was interesting.
Let me work on some cosmetic changes now, such as hover states color change. And then I will work on responsiveness, and making this work on mobile devices.
I am back again. Dude I just added md:flex (which means flex only on medium sizes or above) and text-center to my navbar and I was done! Tailwind's utility classes are mobile-first, so all my classes are technically (and really) valid on phones. Also, I added text-center beacuse I wanted the text to be centered on mobile phones. Does that affect the PC/Desktop CSS? No! Because flex takes over from there.
